221	/// Interprets a `run/execute` Response as the terminal [`Payload`].
222	///
223	/// The success `result` is Norn's versioned stop envelope (`envelope_version: 1`), interpreted by
224	/// [`translate::run_result_to_outcome`]:
225	///
226	/// - `stop.reason == "completed"` with a String `output` → the neutral [`Payload`] is the
227	///   canonical agent-outcome record
228	///   `{"text": <the output string>, "final_message": <the same final output string>,
229	///   "stop_reason": "end_turn", "session_id": <the envelope's own id>}` (the envelope protocol
230	///   defines `output` as the final value; its `stop.reason` is mapped through the neutral vocabulary),
231	/// - `stop.reason == "completed"` with a non-String `output` → [`HarnessError::Contract`]
232	///   naming the canonical agent-outcome contract and what was found — a typed, DETERMINISTIC
233	///   contract refusal at this boundary (terminal in the worker's mapping), never a
234	///   workflow-side serde decode error,
235	/// - any other `stop.reason` → [`HarnessError::Harness`] carrying the whole `stop` object
236	///   (reason plus per-variant detail) verbatim — and the partial `output`, when the envelope
237	///   carried one — so the caller can judge retry (or accept the partial),
238	/// - a `null` result (a prompt that resolved entirely to a local slash command) →
239	///   [`HarnessError::Harness`] — an agent activity must produce output,
240	/// - a result-less, error-less Response (a broken peer) → [`HarnessError::Protocol`],
241	/// - a non-envelope result → [`HarnessError::Protocol`] naming what was missing.
242	///
243	/// An error object on the Response surfaces as [`HarnessError::Harness`].
244	fn response_into_payload(response: JsonRpcResponse) -> Result<Payload, HarnessError> {
245	    if let Some(error) = response.error {
246	        return Err(HarnessError::harness(format!(
247	            "run/execute failed (code {}): {}",
248	            error.code, error.message
249	        )));
250	    }

1	//! NOI-5b transcript subscription: namespace-gated durable-tail + live-splice
2	//! forward loop for one `(workflow, run, activity, attempt)` agent transcript.
3	//!
4	//! This is the agent-observability counterpart to [`super::cluster_stream`]'s
5	//! forward loop, and a NEW ARM on the existing single subscription frame of
6	//! `/events/stream` (the socket stays one-subscription-per-socket; there is no
7	//! multiplexing layer). A client that wants both a workflow stream and a
8	//! transcript opens two `/events/stream` sockets.
9	//!
10	//! # Authorization: namespace-scoped (like the per-workflow event stream)
11	//!
12	//! A transcript belongs to the workflow the activity runs under, so it is
13	//! authorized exactly like the per-workflow event subscription: the caller must
14	//! hold a grant for the transcript's `namespace` AND the target `workflow_id`
15	//! must be visible in it. The gate reuses the SAME
16	//! [`NamespaceGuard::scope`](crate::namespace::NamespaceGuard::scope) +
17	//! [`SubscriptionScope::PerWorkflow`](crate::namespace::SubscriptionScope) path
18	//! the workflow stream uses, so a caller probing a foreign or nonexistent
19	//! workflow receives the guard's anti-leak `not_found`, never a transcript.
20	//!
21	//! # Splice contract (gap-free, no duplicate)
22	//!
23	//! Mirrors the workflow resume path: attach the live broadcast BEFORE reading the
24	//! durable `O` tail (subscribe-then-replay), so an event that races the priming
25	//! read is retained by the receiver and applied after it, deduped on `store_seq`.
26	//! Ephemeral token deltas (`store_seq: None`) are forwarded live and never
27	//! replayed.
28	
29	use aion_core::{ActivityId, RunId};
30	use aion_proto::{
31	    PerWorkflowSubscription, ProtoRunId, ProtoWorkflowId, StreamedActivityEvent,
32	    TranscriptSubscription, WireError,
33	};
